# Era 1, The Exchange: the barn yard and the hands' nights. The yard at dawn (a pleasure before the strap), Kel
# that night, the count with the dogs before the bargain (what Ilo's hiding costs him in the body), and the night
# before the frost list (a pleasure before the list). Story lead, deepening pass, 2026-10-23. DRAFT: every line
# waits for the author's line approval (ledger row S14). No fate is decided here; the deciders stay Josh and George.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the yard at dawn, before the exchange
@node e1.yard.dawn
place: q1_barns
speaker: KETTI
cost: 0
nar: The barns at Haldane's, before it's light. The hands sleep eleven to a barn on straw, and the first one up kicks the fire in the stone ring back to life and puts the black pot on it, and the smell of the oats gets everybody else up.
nar: Ketti Sallis is up first, because she's always up first. She's got her hair tied up in a cloth and her sleeves pushed past the elbow, and her forearms are scratched to the wrist from the stubble, a hundred little lines, some old and some from yesterday.
line: KETTI: Arn. Arn. It's the sun.
line: ARN: It's not the sun. That's the moon.
line: KETTI: It's the sun and you owe me a penny.
line: ARN: I owe you nothing. I tied ninety-one.
line: KETTI: Ninety-four. Old Tesk counted. Ask him.
nar: Old Tesk, on his back in the straw with his hands folded on his chest like a dead bishop, says ninety-four without opening his eyes, and coughs, and turns over.
nar: Arn finds a penny in his shirt and throws it at her and she catches it one-handed without looking, and puts it in her mouth for safekeeping, and goes on stirring the pot.
nar: Davin, eight, is sitting on an upturned bucket by the fire with a crust, and Kel Tesk, fourteen, is sitting on the ground beside him teaching him to whistle through a split stalk of barley. Kel can get a note out of it like a curlew. Davin can get a noise out of it like a door.
line: KEL: You're blowing. Don't blow. Breathe at it.
line: DAVIN: I am breathing at it.
line: KEL: You're blowing at it like it's soup.
nar: Davin tries again and coughs instead, and coughs, and Kel bangs him on the back, not gently, and then takes the stalk off him and does the curlew again, and Davin laughs with his mouth full and sprays crust on the fire.
nar: Up on the end rick, Ilo is already working, with his shirt off in the cold, forking down for the first cart. He's singing. The hands below him come in on the chorus wrong on purpose, and he stops, and looks down at them, and starts it again from the top, slower, like a teacher.
line: KETTI: He'll sing till his teeth drop out, that one.
line: ARN: He'll sing till Furrowe pays him.
line: KETTI: Then he'll sing for ever.
nar: Ketti doles out the oats into the tins, a big spoon each and a little spoon more for Kel, because he's growing, and he looks at the little spoon more and doesn't say anything, and eats it with his fingers before it's cool enough.
voice: undertow: Remember the oats. The little spoon more. {dc: 3}
go: e1.exchange

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Kel, that night
@node e1.kel.night
cost: 0
alt: e1.kel.night.kept ? has(e1.kel_kept)
go: e1.kel.night.walked

@node e1.kel.night.kept
place: q1_barns
speaker: KETTI
cost: 0
nar: That night in the barns. Kel lies on his front in the straw by the fire with his shirt off, because he can't bear it on. His back is six stripes from the shoulder blades to the top of his trousers, gone up in ridges the width of a thumb, and where two of them cross the skin has split and wept and stuck to his shirt, and Ketti had to soak the shirt off him with warm water an inch at a time.
nar: Ketti has a cup of dripping from the yard pot and she's putting it on with two fingers, very lightly. Davin is holding the candle for her, too close, and she keeps pushing his wrist back.
line: KETTI: Hold it still, Davin. I can't see his back for your shaking.
line: DAVIN: Does it hurt?
line: KEL: No.
line: DAVIN: It looks like it hurts.
line: KEL: It's the dripping that hurts. She's put salt in it.
line: KETTI: I have not.
line: KEL: She's put salt in it, Davin. She wants me to sing.
nar: He laughs into the straw. He laughs every time anybody says anything, and the laugh shakes his back, and he stops laughing and holds his breath until it settles, and then somebody says something and he laughs again.
nar: Old Tesk sits against the wall with his knees up, coughing, watching his son's back by the candle. He doesn't come near. He has the barley in his lap in its paper, the double handful, because George Orme said the boy could keep it, being as it was sweepings.
line: TESK: Is it bad, missus.
line: KETTI: It'll be a week before he lies on it. It'll mark.
line: TESK: Mine marked.
nar: He makes the pudding with the barley the next day, the one Kel took it for, and nobody else will have any of it, and he eats it himself, slowly, with Kel watching him.
go: e1.board

@node e1.kel.night.walked
place: q_hill
speaker: TESK
cost: 0
camera: wide
nar: That night, on the hill track above the post. No moon. Kel coming up it in the dark with his bare feet, because the boots are in a paper at the post with the barley, written on. The track is flints and sheep muck and the first frost's not come yet, but the stones are cold enough.
nar: By the turn at the gate his feet are cut on the soles in a dozen places, and every step he puts down leaves a dark mark on the stone that you'd see in the morning if anyone came looking.
nar: Old Tesk is waiting at the gate with a lantern. He's come down from the fold with his chest, which took him an hour, and he's sitting on the wall with the lantern between his feet.
line: TESK: Where's your boots.
line: KEL: At the post. In a bag. They wrote on it.
line: TESK: What did they write.
line: KEL: I don't know. I can't read it, can I.
nar: Old Tesk gets down off the wall and takes his own boots off, one, then the other, and puts them on the stone in front of the boy, and stands there on the flints in his stockings.
line: KEL: I'm not having your boots.
line: TESK: They're too small for me. They've always been too small for me. Put them on.
nar: They go up together, the boy in the boots and the father in his stockings, with the lantern, and the light goes up the hill a turn at a time and goes over the top.
go: e1.board

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the count, before the bargain
@node e1.count
place: q1_barns
speaker: JOSH
cost: 0
nar: The night the rule is passed. Josh Brack comes to the barns at ten with Will and a lantern and the post dog, Pilot, a brindle thing with half an ear, to count every hand who's sleeping on the plain and write them in the book for Mott's list. Every name in the book is a name on the list at the frost.
nar: Ilo isn't in the book. He's been sleeping in Furrowe's loft since the barley, and a man in Furrowe's loft is nobody's business. But Furrowe hasn't said anything yet, and Ilo doesn't know if he will, and a man with no paper who's found in the barns at a count is a hand.
nar: So he's in the ditch behind the end barn, in the dark, under the hawthorn, lying flat in a foot of black water with the cold coming up through his shirt. He's been there since he heard the gate.
nar: He gave his bread at supper to Davin, because Davin was coughing and eats when he's coughing. So he's had a cup of the oats at five in the morning and nothing since, and his stomach has gone past growling into a slow ache like a fist pressed in under the ribs, and the cold is getting into the ache and making it worse.
line: JOSH: Pruett. Five. Where's the grandmother?
line: WILL: She's in the corner, Dad. She's asleep.
line: JOSH: Wake her up. I'm not counting her by the smell.
nar: The water's in his ears. He can hear the count through it, name by name, and Pilot snuffling along the barn wall, and the dog's chain. His feet went numb half an hour ago and he can't feel if they're still in his boots. His jaw keeps going and he holds it shut with his hand so his teeth won't click.
nar: Pilot comes round the end of the barn. Ilo hears him stop.
alt: e1.count.josh ? q_class == militia
go: e1.count.dog

@node e1.count.josh
place: q1_barns
speaker: JOSH
cost: 0
nar: The dog has stopped at the end of the barn with his head down at the ditch and the hair standing up along his back. He makes the low noise he makes before he goes in.
voice: ballast: A man in a ditch at a count is a man who knows he shouldn't be counted. That's what the dog's for. {dc: 2}
voice: grip: Call him off and Will's watching you call him off. {dc: 3}
opt: "Let him go in." -> e1.count.dog {do: set(e1.dog_loosed)}
opt: "Whistle him back. Say it: Pilot. Here. It's rats." -> e1.count.called {do: set(e1.dog_called)}

@node e1.count.called
place: q1_barns
speaker: JOSH
cost: 0
line: JOSH: Pilot. Here. It's rats.
nar: The dog doesn't come at once. He stands with his head down at the hawthorn, and Josh whistles again, sharp, and the dog turns and comes back to his boot with his tail down.
line: WILL: There's no rats in water, Dad.
line: JOSH: There's rats in everything. Get the lantern.
nar: They go. Ilo lies in the ditch another hour after the gate shuts, to be sure, and when he gets up he can't stand, and he crawls the last of it to Furrowe's yard on his hands and knees through the frost-wet grass with his boots full of water. Getting out through the hawthorn he's ripped his left forearm open from the wrist to the elbow on a branch, and he doesn't feel it till he sees it black in the yard.
go: e1.loft

@node e1.count.dog
place: q1_barns
speaker: JOSH
cost: 0
nar: The dog goes in. He goes into the hawthorn and into the water, and gets Ilo by the forearm, the left, through the shirt, and pulls.
nar: Ilo doesn't shout. He gets his right hand round the dog's muzzle and holds it shut with the dog's teeth still in his arm, and lies there in the water holding it, and the dog shakes its head to get a better grip, and every time it shakes he can feel the teeth go further in, and he holds it.
nar: It's quiet, the whole thing. The water slaps. That's all.
line: JOSH: Pilot. What's he got. {when: q_class == inspector}
line: WILL: Rats, Dad. He's got a rat. {when: q_class == inspector}
line: JOSH: Pilot. Out. What've you got. {when: has(e1.dog_loosed)}
line: WILL: It'll be rats, Dad. It's a ditch. {when: has(e1.dog_loosed)}
nar: Josh whistles. The dog lets go, the way a trained dog lets go, all at once, and backs out of the hawthorn with its muzzle wet and dark, and goes to his boot.
nar: The lantern comes to the end of the barn, and stops, and swings once over the ditch. Will is holding it. He looks straight at the hawthorn for a long time. Then he turns round with it.
line: WILL: Nothing, Dad. It's a rat hole.
nar: Ilo stays in the water an hour after the gate shuts. When he gets up he can't stand, and he crawls the last of it to Furrowe's yard on his hands and knees, with his left arm held up against his chest because there's a flap of skin on it the size of a penny that opens and shuts when he moves his fingers.
go: e1.loft

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the night before the frost list
@node e1.frost.eve
place: q1_barns
speaker: KETTI
cost: 0
nar: The Monday night, the night before the frost, though nobody knows that yet. In the barns at Haldane's. Somebody has got potatoes, a whole sack, off Orme's ground where they were left in the ruts, and they're in the fire in their skins, and the barn smells like nothing else in the world.
nar: Ketti is sitting with Davin's coat across her knees, putting a patch on the elbow from a bit of Arn's old shirt. She bites the thread off. Davin is asleep against her side with his mouth open, and every so often he coughs in his sleep without waking, and she puts her hand on his back till he stops.
nar: Ilo has come over from Furrowe's for the potatoes, with his arm in a strip of sacking. Nobody's asked him about it. He sits by the fire and pokes the potatoes out of the ash with a stick and juggles them from hand to hand, and blows on them, and hands them round.
line: ILO: Ketti. That one's yours. It's the best one.
line: KETTI: They're all the same.
line: ILO: That one's the best. I've been watching it.
line: KETTI: You've been watching a potato.
line: ILO: Since it went in. Somebody had to.
nar: She splits it with her thumbs and the steam comes up into her face, and she shuts her eyes in it.
line: KETTI: My mam used to do them on the hill in the wall. In the hot stones. You'd put them in at dinner and they'd be done by dark and you'd have forgotten them.
line: ILO: Mine as well.
line: KETTI: Did yours burn them?
line: ILO: Every time. She'd say it was the stones.
line: KETTI: It's never the stones.
nar: Arn says when they go up, at the frost, he'll make her a wall like that at the hut, and she says he said that last year, and he says he'll make it this year, and she says he said that last year as well.
nar: Ketti sings, after. Not loud, not for anybody, a Heshen song about a girl who loses a goat and finds it on the roof, and the barn goes quiet to hear it, and two or three come in on the end where the goat comes down.
nar: There's a white skin on the water bucket by the door when Ilo goes out. He looks at it. He puts his finger through it, and it breaks like paper.
go: e1.frost
